    "(none)" : {

    },
    "**Discard Changes** puts a modified snapshot back to its committed version; on a suite it does so for every modified snapshot in it. **Discard All…**, beside the filter chips (or **Snapshot ▸ Discard All Changes in Project…**), does it for the whole project after asking first. Untracked snapshots are left alone. A change that was never committed exists nowhere but in GimDiff's undo: **Edit ▸ Undo** (⌘Z) puts it back until you open another project or quit." : {

    },
    "**File ▸ Open Recent** lists the same folders as the recent list. Hover over a folder in the list and click its **×**, or right-click it, to forget it or reveal it in the Finder; **Clear Recent Projects** forgets them all. The **×** beside the project's name (or **File ▸ Close Project**) goes back to the list." : {

    },
    "**Paste** (⌘V, or the **Paste** button in the toolbar) works the same way. Copy a failure log from Xcode or Swift Testing and GimDiff reads the image paths out of it; copy files in the Finder and GimDiff opens them. A GitHub pull request link opens that pull request." : {

    },
    "**Side by Side** shows the two images next to each other. **Split View** draws both in one frame under a split you drag across it. **Onion Skin** lays the candidate over the baseline; drag its slider to fade from one to the other, and whatever moves is what changed. **Diff Mask** paints every changed pixel in magenta and reports how much of the image changed. Switch between them from the toolbar or the **View** menu. **View ▸ Onion Skin** jumps the fade to either image or back to both equally, as the slider's **Reset** does." : {

    },
    "100% Match" : {

    },
    "@%@" : {

    },
    "A link, owner/repo#123, or a number from the open project's repository." : {

    },
    "A pair pasted from a failure log, dropped in, or opened by URL offers **Keep Baseline** and **Keep Candidate** instead. Keeping the baseline writes nothing; keeping the candidate copies it over the baseline. Either way the baseline is left on screen on its own." : {

    },
    "A project opens on its own status. Switch the sidebar from **Project** to **Test Run** (or View ▸ Test Run) to read the newest run Xcode left in DerivedData; the menu under the switch (or View ▸ Test Runs) lists every run Xcode still keeps, to look at an older one instead. The sidebar lists only the snapshots the run failed on, and selecting one shows the image the test rendered against the recorded file. Use **Diff Mask** to see where they differ. **Accept** re-records the snapshot without running the test again, and **Accept All…** beside the run picker (or **Snapshot ▸ Accept All Test Run Failures…**) does so for every failure at once, after asking. When a test attached the snapshot more than once, click the candidate's file name (or use **Snapshot ▸ Test Run Candidate**) to choose which rendering to compare. Switch back to **Project** at any time; the run stays read, and a new one is picked up as soon as Xcode finishes writing it — unless you picked an older run, which stays until you pick the newest again." : {

    },
